# EL-2 — our own outbound EchoLink audio, sequence 0..7.
#
# Provenance: same capture and same QSO as `live-proxy-audio-in.hex`; see that
# file's header for the capture, the alignment argument and the RTP oddities.
# Cited by SHA-256, never by path.
#
# Our own half, checked in because it is what EL-7's serialiser is tested
# against: these are the octets a working client must produce. They contain our
# own speech, encoded, and no credential — the proxy digest is in the unframed
# login, not here.
#
# What it pins beyond the inbound fixture: a sender may start its sequence at
# zero and emit SSRC zero, which is what this client did. Neither is a protocol
# rule — the wider capture set has a peer sending SSRC 1787057786 and sequences
# beginning at 2126 — so a decoder must not key on either. This fixture is the
# "what we emit" half of that pair, not a statement about what is permitted.
# Machine-generated. The captures themselves are not in the repository —
# they are the maintainer's own, and large. Regenerate with:
#   scripts/pcap-to-fixture.py <capture> --transport tcp --stream 0 --dir out --range 158:165 --assume-aligned
# Capture SHA-256: 2a8aa1ba78218170fcf3cf4c7a45c6c67fd3541192cdab895307f6a16699cd01
#
# Each line is one EchoLink proxy frame verbatim, reassembled from the
# TCP stream: the 9-byte header — type(1), peer IPv4(4), length(4,
# LITTLE-endian) — and its payload. `[n]` is the frame's index in
# arrival order across both directions, so a gap in the indices is a
# frame deliberately left out.

# [158] 16.423s ==> UDP DATA    peer 13.57.14.183   144B  RTP v3 pt3 seq     0 ts 0 ssrc 0  4 GSM
